ngle it makes with the tangent at one end is equal to the angle it subtends at the circumference? No, that's for the same chord.\n\nLet's use the unfolding method or the property of the central angle. Let the ray hit the circle at $A_0 = P$, then $A_1, A_2, \n.cdots, A_k = P$. The central angle $\theta_i$ corresponding to the arc $A_i A_{i+1}$ depends on the incidence angle. Let $\beta$ be the angle between the ray and the tangent. The angle between the radius $OA_i$ and the ray $A_i A_{i+1}$ is $90^\to - \beta$. The triangle $O A_i A_{i+1}$ is isosceles with $OA_i = OA_{i+1} = R$. The angle at $O$ is $\theta_i$. The base angles are $(180^\to - \theta_i)/2 = 90^\to - \theta_i/2$. So $90^\to - \beta = 90^\to - \theta_i/2 \nRightarrow \beta = \theta_i/2$. Thus, the central angle subtended by the chord is $2\beta$.\n\nThis is a crucial insight: the central angle of the arc between consecutive bounce points is $2\beta$, where $\beta$ is the angle between the ray and the tangent.\n\nSo, if the laser hits at $P$ (angle 0), the first hit $A_1$ will be at central angle $2\beta_1 = 2n^\to$. The next hit $A_2$ will be at $2n^\to + 2n^\to = 4n^\to$, and so on, up to $A_k$ at $k \times 2n^\to$. For the beam to return to $P$, the total rotation must be an integer multiple of $360^\to$. So $k \times 2n$ must be a multiple of $360$. The minimum number of points illuminated is $k+1$ (including start and end, but the problem says \"illuminating the points they hit... until they reach P again. (P is also illuminated at the end.)\". The points are $P, A_1, \n.cdots, A_{k-1}, P$. The distinct points are $P, A_1, \n.cdots, A_{k-1}$. Wait, if it returns to P, the set of points is $\n . { P, A_1, \n . \n cdots, A_{k-1} }$. The number of distinct points is $k$? Or does the sequence include the final P? The question asks for the number of illuminated points. The set is $S = \n . { \text{hit points} }$. The ray starts at $P$, hits $A_1 \n . . . A_k = P$. The set of points is $\n . \n cdots P$. The number of unique points is $k$ if the period is $k$ and $A_i \n replicate A_j$. Actually, if it returns to P after $k$ bounces, the sequence is $P, A_1, \n cdots A_{k-1}, P$. The distinct points are $P, A_1, \n cdots A_{k-1}$, so $k$ points. But wait, if $k=1$, we go $P \to P$. That means the ray goes into the wall and comes back? No, it hits the wall. If $2n = 360$, $n=180$, which is not allowed ($n < 90$). So $k > 1$. The points are $P, A_1, \n . A_{k-1}$. There are $k$ distinct points? Wait. If $k=2$, $P \to A_1 \to P$. Points: $P, A_1$. Count = 2. If $k=3$, $P \to A_1 \to A_2 \to P$. Points: $P, A_1, A_2$. Count = 3. So the answer is $k$.\n\nThe condition is $k \times n$ is a multiple of $180$. $k \times n \n . = m \times 180$. We want the smallest $k$. So $k = 180 / \text{gcd}(n, 180)$. But we must also consider the second laser with angle $(n+1)^\to$. Let $k_1$ be the number of points for angle $n^\to$, $k_2$ for $(n+1)^\to$. The total number of illuminated points is the size of the union of the two sets of points. Since the geometry is circular and symmetric, the points for the first laser form a regular polygon with $k_1$ vertices. The points for the second laser form a regular polygon with $k_2$ vertices. Both polygons share the vertex $P$ (at angle 0). The total number of distinct points is $|Set_1 \n \n cdots Set_2| = k_1 + k_2 - |Set_1 \n cdots Set_2|$. To minimize this, we need to maximize the overlap, or rather, find $n$ such that the sum is minimized.\n\nWait, the problem says \"illuminating the points they hit... until they reach P again\". This describes a single trajectory for each laser. The set of points for laser 1 is a regular $k_1$-gon. The set for laser 2 is a regular $k_2$-gon. Both contain 0 (the angle of P). The vertices are at angles $j \times (360/k_1)$ for laser 1, and $j \times (360/k_2)$ for laser 2, modulo 360. We need to minimize $k_1 + k_2 - |intersection|$. The intersection size is the number of common vertices. Since $P$ is in both, the intersection is at least 1. The vertices of the $k_1$-gon are the subgroup generated by $360/k_1$ in $SO(2)$ (or rather the cyclic group $C_{k_1}$). Similarly for $k_2$. The intersection is the subgroup generated by $360/\text{lcm}(k_1, k_2)$. The number of common points is $\text{gcd}(k_1, k_2)$. So the number of distinct points is $k_1 + k_2 - \text{gcd}(k_1, k_2) = k_1 + k_2(1 - 1/\text{gcd}(k_1, k_2)) = k_1 + k_2 - \text{gcd}(k_1, k_2)$. This is equal to $\text{lcm}(k_1, k_2) \n . \times \n \n . \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \