  "next_thought": "The problem asks for the smallest number of sides $n$ of the orthogonal projection of a regular dodecahedron onto a plane. \n\nA regular dodecahedron has 12 faces, which are regular pentagons. It also has 20 vertices and 30 edges.\n\nWhen projecting a convex polyhedron orthogonally onto a plane, the image is a convex polygon. The vertices of this polygon are the projections of the vertices of the polyhedron that lie on the 'silhouette' (the boundary of the projection). These are the vertices of the 'equator' of the polyhedron relative to the projection direction.\n\nThe number of vertices of the projected polygon corresponds to the number of edges connecting the 'front' vertices to the 'back' vertices in the projection. More simply, the silhouette is formed by the edges that separate faces that are 'visible' from those that are 'hidden'.\n\nThe possible projections of a regular dodecahedron are classified by the orientation of the projection axis relative to the symmetry axes of the dodecahedron. The symmetry axes of a regular dodecahedron are:\n1. Face centers (5-fold symmetry)\n2. Vertex centers (3-fold symmetry)\n3. Edge centers (2-fold symmetry)\n\nCase 1: Projection along a 5-fold axis (perpendicular to a face).\nIn this orientation, one face is perpendicular to the viewing direction (appears as a regular pentagon). The opposite face is parallel to it and appears as a concentric regular pentagon inside. The 5-fold symmetry suggests the silhouette will be an ellipse-like shape or a polygon with 10 vertices? Let's think. The vertices of the dodecahedron can be grouped. The face perpendicular to the axis has 5 vertices. The opposite face has 5 vertices. The other faces are 'band' faces around the equator. There are 10 such faces. The vertices involved in the 'equatorial' band would form the silhouette. Actually, the projection of a regular pentagon (face) along its normal is a pentagon. The outer boundary of the projection is determined by the o