xis.\n# Then I(T2) = I(T1). This works!\n# So the two triangles are reflections of each other across the line OI.\n# But wait, if T2 is reflection of T1, their intersection is the part of T1 symmetric to itself? No.\n# Intersection of T and Refl(T).\n# Let's compute Area(T1 n T2).\n\n# Define T1\n# Line1: normal angle theta - delta.\n# Theta = atan2(0, 3sqrt(5)) = 0.\n# delta = acos(6 / 3sqrt(5)) = acos(2/sqrt(5)).\ntheta = 0\ndelta = math.acos(2/math.sqrt(5))\n# Normal angles: -delta, +delta.\n# Line1 normal: -delta. Direction: perpendicular.\n# A1 = (R cos(-delta+90), R sin(-delta+90))? No.\n# Line through origin with normal at -delta.\n# Point on line at angle -delta + 90.\nalpha1 = -delta + math.pi/2\nx1, y1 = R * math.cos(alpha1), R * math.sin(alpha1)\nP1 = (x1, y1)\np2 = (-x1, -y1)\n# C1: reflection of C_top? No, C1 is found by intersection.\n# But we know T2 is reflection of T1.\n# So C2 = reflection of C1 across x-axis.\n# So T2 vertices: A1, B1 (same as T1 if line is same?)\n# Wait, if T2 is reflection of T1, then line AB for T2 is reflection of line AB for T1.\n# So they share the same line AB? No.\n# Line1 and Line2 are distinct.\n# They are symmetric across x-axis.\n# So T1 has hypotenuse L1. T2 has hypotenuse L2.\n# L2 is reflection of L1.\n# So T2 is reflection of T1 across x-axis.\n# Intersection of T1 and Refl(T1).\n# Since T1 is symmetric? No.\n# But the setup is symmetric.\n# Let's compute vertices of T1.\n# Line L1: normal -delta. Line eq: x cos(-delta) + y sin(-delta) = 0.\n# C1 is intersection of x^2+y^2=R^2 and line through I at distance r/\sin45.\n# We can just take C1 from the quadratic solution.\n# Then C2 = (x1, -y1).\n# T1 = (P1, P2, C1). T2 = (Ref(P1), Ref(P2), Ref(C1)).\n# Since P1, P2 are symmetric across x-axis? No, P1 is on L1, P2=-P1.\n# Ref(P1) = P1^x, P1^y? No, (x,y)->(x,-y).\n# L2 normal +delta.\n# So T2 hypotenuse is reflection of T1 hypotenuse.\n# T2 = Ref(T1).\n\n# Compute area of T1 n T2.\n\n# Solve C for L1\n# Ix = 3*sqrt(5),